from review import card,save,DATE
i='TCS-4754';_,c=card(i);refs=c['references'];refs[0]['locator']='Section 1.1.3, Question 12: unconditional computational zero knowledge for some gap MCSP or MINKT; Section 2.3: relativization limitation.'
refs += [dict(id='full',title='A Relativization Perspective on Meta-Complexity — full version',authors='Hanlin Ren; Rahul Santhanam',year=2021,url='https://eccc.weizmann.ac.il/report/2021/089/',pdf_url='https://eccc.weizmann.ac.il/report/2021/089/download',locator='ECCC TR21-089, 25 June 2021. Question 1.12 and Section 1.2.3; Section 2.1 distinguishes circuits, Levin Kt and unary-time-bounded Kolmogorov complexity.'),dict(id='average',title='Robustness of Average-Case Meta-Complexity via Pseudorandomness',authors='Rahul Ilango; Hanlin Ren; Rahul Santhanam',year=2022,url='https://doi.org/10.1145/3519935.3520051',pdf_url='https://hanlin-ren.github.io/files/pdf/stoc22_robustness.pdf',locator='STOC 2022, pages 1575–1583. Theorem 2.7: infinitely-often average-case computational zero knowledge; the MCSP result assumes a locally samplable distribution.'),dict(id='owf',title='One-Way Functions and Zero Knowledge',authors='Shuichi Hirahara; Mikito Nanashima',year=2026,url='https://doi.org/10.1137/24M1689971',pdf_url='https://eccc.weizmann.ac.il/report/2024/063/download',locator='SIAM Journal on Computing, published online 26 January 2026, following STOC 2024. Publisher abstract and publication history checked. Detailed model and Theorem 2.1 read in full ECCC TR24-063, 6 April 2024: Section 4.1 uses a strict upper no-threshold; Definitions 4.1–4.2 define proofs and zero knowledge. This link is the 2024 author version, not the full 2026 publisher PDF.')]
save(i,dict(title='Unconditional zero knowledge for gap circuit complexity',formal=r'Is there a fixed rational \(\varepsilon\in(0,1/2)\) for which \(\mathrm{Gap}_{\varepsilon}\mathrm{MCSP}\) admits a computational zero-knowledge interactive proof system without a computational hardness assumption? Given the length-\(N=2^n\) truth table of a Boolean function, the promise distinguishes circuit size at most \(N^\varepsilon\) from size greater than \(N^{1-\varepsilon}\). This card selects the MCSP branch of the original question and the gap family used by Hirahara and Nanashima; it does not claim equivalence with every MINKT or other gap variant.',definitions=r'''The common input is the full truth table \(x\) of a function \(f:\{0,1\}^n\to\{0,1\}\), with \(n\geq1\) and \(N=2^n\). Let \(C(f)\) be its minimum Boolean circuit size. Fix the standard basis of binary AND and OR and unary NOT, with constant inputs available, arbitrary fan-out, one output, and size equal to the number of gates. Circuits are acyclic and compute every truth-table entry correctly.

A yes-instance satisfies \(C(f)\leq N^\varepsilon\). A no-instance satisfies \(C(f)>N^{1-\varepsilon}\). Inputs between these thresholds have no required decision outcome. The strict no-threshold follows the formal definition in the later source; the thresholds need not be integers. The constant \(\varepsilon\) is chosen once, not supplied per instance. The task has both low-complexity and high-complexity promised inputs at arbitrarily large lengths.

An interactive proof has an honest prover \(P\) and a randomized verifier \(V\). Total communication, rounds and verifier computation are polynomial in \(N\), the explicit truth-table length. No computational bound is imposed on the prover. On every yes-instance, the honest interaction accepts with probability at least \(2/3\). On every no-instance, every cheating prover, including an unbounded one, causes acceptance with probability at most \(1/3\). This is a proof system; soundness only against efficient provers would be an argument system.

Computational zero knowledge requires that for every randomized polynomial-time verifier \(V^*\), including one given arbitrary polynomial-length auxiliary information \(z\), there is a randomized polynomial-time simulator \(S_{V^*}\). Given only \(x,z\), it produces a distribution computationally indistinguishable from the entire view of \(V^*\) in the honest-prover interaction on \(x\). That view includes the common input, auxiliary information, verifier randomness, messages and final output. The simulator is not given a small circuit or other witness.

Indistinguishability is required on all yes-instances: every polynomial-size distinguishing circuit has only negligible advantage in \(N\), uniformly over these instances and polynomial-length auxiliary strings. Negligible means smaller than \(N^{-c}\) for every fixed positive \(c\) at sufficiently large \(N\). The simulator and verifier use the ordinary polynomial-time convention of the later source. No setup, random oracle, trusted third party or hardness assumption is supplied.

The chosen circuit basis fixes a concrete representation. The existential constant-gap formulation tolerates constant-factor changes of standard finite bases by choosing a slightly wider gap and handling finitely many small lengths separately. It does not identify circuits with a shortest program: MINKT instead bounds the program length needed to print a string within a supplied time budget, and its gap parameters constitute a separate choice.

The target asks for one protocol meeting worst-case completeness, soundness and zero knowledge at every input length. A protocol that succeeds on most inputs from a particular distribution or only at infinitely many lengths is a weaker result.''',question_type='yes_no',answer_criterion='Give some fixed admissible gap constant and a protocol with proofs of polynomial verifier and simulator cost, completeness, soundness against all provers, and computational zero knowledge against arbitrary efficient auxiliary-input verifiers, without assuming one-way functions or another hardness premise. A negative answer must rule out the specified protocols for every constant in this selected gap family. Failure for that family would not by itself refute all alternatives in the original broader question.',context_blocks=[dict(title='A small circuit is easy to verify but may reveal information',text='Yes-instances have polynomially checkable small-circuit witnesses, so this is a promise problem in NP. Revealing the entire witness supplies an ordinary proof, but does not establish zero knowledge.',citation='owf'),dict(title='Hardness assumptions already give protocols',text='The standard one-way-function theorem supplies zero-knowledge proofs for NP. The original question asks for an unconditional result; the case where the problem is worst-case hard but suitable cryptographic hardness is unavailable is not covered by that theorem.',citation='primary'),dict(title='Unconditional average-case progress has weaker quantifiers',text='Ilango, Ren and Santhanam obtain infinitely-often average-case protocols, with locally samplable distributions in their MCSP statement. That does not give a protocol correct on every promised input at every length.',citation='average'),dict(title='The later equivalence retains a separate hardness premise',text='Hirahara and Nanashima characterize one-way functions using both membership of the gap problem in CZK and worst-case hardness of CZK. The theorem does not establish the gap problem’s unconditional CZK membership. Their 2024 full version also distinguishes errorless average-case schemes from error-prone ones.',citation='owf')],why='Would provide a natural unconditional zero-knowledge protocol for a central problem about circuit descriptions. It probes the connection between worst-case complexity, average-case cryptographic hardness and verification that reveals no efficiently usable information beyond the claim.',progress=[dict(date='2022-03',text='The source asks for unconditional zero knowledge for some gap MCSP or time-bounded Kolmogorov problem.',citation='primary'),dict(date='2022-06',text='STOC work gives infinitely-often average-case zero-knowledge protocols, with the stated distribution restrictions.',citation='average'),dict(date='2024-04',text='The full author paper states a worst-case characterization involving gap MCSP membership in CZK together with a separate hardness condition.',citation='owf'),dict(date='2026-01-26',text='The journal version of the characterization is published; its abstract retains both conditions and does not announce unconditional gap MCSP membership.',citation='owf')],references=refs,status='open',status_note='Read Question 12 and the ECCC full source, STOC 2022 Theorem 2.7, and full ECCC 2024 Theorem 2.1 with Definitions 4.1–4.2; checked the January 2026 publisher abstract and dates. Searches through 12 September 2026 found no unconditional worst-case resolution. The MCSP branch and the later source’s fixed power-gap family are explicit editorial scope choices, not a claimed equivalence with all original alternatives. The 2026 full publisher proof was not read.',model_self_contained=True,requires_context=False,review_note='Replaced the placeholder with an explicit, source-grounded MCSP gap branch. Defined full truth-table input, thresholds, proof soundness, simulation and security quantifiers. Preserved the broader original source sentence and distinguished all-input protocols from average-case and assumption-based progress.',importance=dict(score=c['importance']['score'],method='editorial',reason='A structural zero-knowledge question connecting circuit meta-complexity with the difference between worst-case hardness and cryptographic assumptions. Existing score retained.',assessed_on=DATE),working_summary=dict(sentences=['The input is a complete truth table promised to have either a small or a much larger minimum circuit.','The selected question asks for some fixed gap admitting an unconditional computational zero-knowledge proof.','The verifier is efficient, while soundness must hold even against an unbounded cheating prover.','All promised inputs and all input lengths must satisfy the guarantees.','Average-case protocols and the later one-way-function characterization do not establish this membership.'],basis='saved_sources',source_basis='source-text',written_on=DATE,source_refs=['primary','average','owf'])),notes=['Explicitly selected the MCSP alternative and the fixed power-gap family used by later primary work.','Defined the full truth-table input, circuit basis and strict upper no-threshold.','Separated interactive proofs from arguments and malicious-verifier simulation from honest-verifier privacy.','Checked infinitely-often average-case progress and the January 2026 characterization without dropping their qualifications.'],checked_sources=[r['url'] for r in refs]+['https://eccc.weizmann.ac.il/report/2024/063/'])
